+\subsection{Complete support for the modeled character domain}
+\label{sec:character-support}
+A finite search alphabet is sound only when it represents every character distinction made by the analyzed languages. The earlier implementation collected input literals and searched a fixed preferred pool for a fresh character. When every preferred character was already literal, its fallback retained that same pool. A character outside the pool could still match a wildcard, so the fallback omitted a nonempty semantic class. Analyzer/replayer agreement could not detect the common omission. The correction changes support construction in the retained core, rather than narrowing the scalar matcher to the deficient alphabet.
+
+We make the domain explicit: $\Gamma=\{\mathrm{chr}(k)\mid 0\leq k<\mathtt{0x110000}\}$, the Python string code-point domain~\cite{python-unicode}. The model does not normalize Unicode, match grapheme clusters, or interpret a code point as a UTF-8 byte. This is not a statement that a provider issues every modeled string. The strict packet transport separately requires UTF-8-encodable input; an isolated surrogate in a packet yields an invalid-input \Unknown{}. Direct-library tests may nevertheless exercise such Python strings to check the primitive domain without claiming they are valid provider tokens.
+
+Let $S$ contain every literal character in the issuer, policy and intent, together with every fixed constructor literal and the boundaries of its character predicates. In this implementation, the default owner/repository predicate excludes slash, colon, star and question mark; the suffix predicate excludes colon, star and question mark. These boundary characters are singleton classes even when a policy only uses star and question mark as operators. In \code{StringEquals} they are also ordinary literal characters. Define
+\begin{equation}
+A=S\cup\{r\}\quad\text{where }r\in\Gamma\setminus S,
+\qquad
+\pi(c)=\begin{cases}c,&c\in S,\\r,&c\notin S.\end{cases}
+\label{eq:complete-character-support}
+\end{equation}
+When $S=\Gamma$, set $A=S$ and $\pi$ to the identity. Extend $\pi$ to strings by applying it to each character. Preferred characters only influence the concrete choice of $r$; exhausting their pool does not remove the residual class. The revised selector continues over a disjoint enumeration of the complete code-point domain. It may omit a residual representative only when that domain is actually exhausted.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Support completeness for the implemented predicates]
+\label{prop:character-support}
+Suppose $S$ contains the literal singleton tests and every finite exclusion used by the constructor automata, and every other consuming transition is unrestricted. For each issuer, equality, or star/question-mark language $L$ built from these predicates, $x\in L$ if and only if $\pi(x)$ is accepted by its restriction to $A$. Consequently, support restriction preserves the emptiness and containment judgments used by the effective subject/audience analysis.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+For a singleton predicate $c=a$, the tested character $a$ belongs to $S$. A character outside $S$ cannot equal $a$, and neither can its representative. An excluded-character predicate has its complete exclusion set in $S$; all characters outside $S$, including $r$, therefore have identical membership. An unrestricted transition accepts both, and an epsilon transition consumes neither. Thus replacing an input character by its representative preserves every enabled transition. Induction on the length of a run establishes both directions of NFA membership; epsilon closure does not alter the argument.
+
+Union, intersection and complement-relative-to-$\Gamma^*$ preserve this equivalence, as does difference. Apply the same map separately to the subject and audience coordinates. Every concrete effective-admission violation therefore has a representative violation, while every representative violation is itself a concrete string pair in the declared domain. This proves the required emptiness and containment equivalences. The argument depends on the stated primitive predicates: adding a new character class or normalization rule requires refining the partition, not merely reusing this theorem.
+\end{proof}
+
+The low-level NFA API remains a finite-alphabet API. Its membership operation takes representative words; it does not silently reinterpret an arbitrary out-of-support character as a literal. Concrete membership is evaluated either by the scalar matcher or by explicitly constructing the support and applying $\pi$. The analyzer and effective replayer add the constructor boundaries before search and check coverage before interpreting a result. Failure to establish coverage is an error at the backend boundary and hence insufficient evidence at the strict gate, not a safety result.
+
+The atomic certificate verifier now checks the declared domain, every required singleton, and the nonempty residual class before checking witness or invariant transitions. The residual check uses the domain cardinality and the supplied singleton set; it does not call the fresh-character selector. Atomic certificates use version 3 and effective-case certificates use version 6. Earlier versions require regeneration. A full-review receipt additionally binds the changed implementation identity, so a historical receipt cannot retain a positive judgment merely because its producer and replayer previously shared the same incomplete support.
 
 \subsection{Independent scalar evaluation of positive tuples}
 For each required tuple, the scalar checker first asks whether it is mintable under the selected profile and intended by the contract. It then evaluates every supported statement against the tuple and takes the disjunction of matching Allows minus any matching Deny. The check records the three Boolean components separately, along with the tuple's reason. A record outside the issuer domain or intent is classified as invalid even if the policy happens to reject it.
